This is my attempt at duplicating the "cloudfront with S3 bucket" example
that AWS offers at https://github.com/aws-samples/amazon-cloudfront-secure-static-site/tree/master/templates 

I would like to think this is both clearer and better explained.  In terms of the "better explained", I challenge myself to write a lot of commentary.

This is the target that creates everything - and can also strip it down if run as "--teardown"

	target cloudfront_website_from_bucket

The core of the solution is to have a bucket which has all of the files in it that we want

		ensure aws.S3.Bucket "news.consolidator.info" => consolidator_bucket
			@teardown delete

We need to copy some files into that, so we assume that there are files in a directory
indicated by ${CONSOLIDATOR_SRC}/IMAGE.  We find that directory (if it exists) and copy
the files into the bucket.

Get the value of the CONSOLIDATOR_SRC variable.  This will be a string

		env "CONSOLIDATOR_SRC" => root

We can now find the directory corresponding to that, which is also an active "file source",
so we can use it as the source of files for a copy action.

		files.dir root "IMAGE" => src_dir

Now copy the files from the src dir to the bucket.

		files.copy src_dir consolidator_bucket

Turning to the other end, it's a requirement that we have a domain name that we can use.  A lot of the example seems to revolve around whether
or not you want the domain to respond, or whether you just want a subdomain to respond (they call this "apex" which is apparently
official DNS terminology - the "root" domain).

		find aws.Route53.DomainName "consolidator.news" => domain

The domain needs a certificate.  In addition to the "www" subdomain, we want to create
an alias for the "apex" node.

This is touch-and-go complexity for doing something using ensure rather than stepping up to adding
a specific "certificate" verb.  The big clue is in the duplication.

		ensure aws.CertificateManager.Certificate "www.consolidator.news" => cert
			@teardown delete
			Domain <- domain
			SubjectAlternativeNames <- "consolidator.news" // The apex node
			ValidationMethod <- "DNS"

Now we are in a position to set up our cloudfront distribution, which will serve content on the named
site from the bucket we created earlier.

		ensure aws.CloudFront.Distribution "for-consolidator-news" => cloudfront
			@teardown delete

In order to use the bucket with cloudfront, we need to say that we have a policy in place on the bucket
I think it's reasonable to do this in two steps; I think it may also be reasonable to allow it to be done
in a single step, but let's start with the more flexible approach

//		policy "allow-cloudfront-to-access-bucket"
//			principal "cloudfront.amazonaws.com"
//			action "s3:GetObject"

I also think it's reasonable, given that we need this, that it all happens in one step.
I think I want a "cloudfront" step at this point, quite apart from the fact that I want to build one.

		policy => cloudfront_policy
			allow aws.action.S3.GetObject (consolidator_bucket->allResources)
				action aws.action.S3.PutObject
				principal aws.principal.Service aws.principal.CloudFront
				condition aws.cond.StringEquals aws.SourceArn (cloudfront->arn)

		ensure aws.IAM.Policy "allow-cloudfront-to-access-bucket"
			@teardown delete
			Policy <- cloudfront_policy